NAME
====

**wattle-projects** - Wattle project layout and declarations

DESCRIPTION
===========

A Wattle *project* is a directory with an ``info.edn`` file at its root. A
project may include Wattle source, Zig source, tests and documentation. Its
info file declares three kinds of *artifact*: executables, libraries and
documentation.

An *executable* is a program, a *library* provides modules that programs import
and *documentation* describes the project or its programs. A project may
declare any combination of these artifacts. An *application* is a project with
executables for installation. See `wattle-applications(7)`.

This page describes the project layout and declarations.

LAYOUT
======

As described above, Wattle projects require the ``info.edn`` file to be
located in the project root. The source files and documentation may be arranged
as the project author prefers. That said, project authors should be aware that
several commands use particular directories:

- `.wattle/`:
  Libraries copied or fetched by `wattle-build(1)` are placed here for use
  during development. This directory is also searched for bare imports.
  `wattle-build(1)` creates a `.wattle/.gitignore` that excludes its contents.
  These generated files should not be committed.

- `test/`:
  `wattle-test(1)` runs each Wattle file under this directory. Most project authors
  will want to place tests in this directory.

- `zig-out/`:
  The default output directory for Zig builds. `wattle-build(1)` places
  generated executables, native libraries and web programs here. Native
  libraries are in `zig-out/lib/`, which is searched for bare imports during
  development.

`wattle-run(1)` and `wattle-check(1)` use the info file beside the script,
without searching parent directories. `wattle-build(1)` and `wattle-test(1)`
use the info file in the current directory. Running a program does not copy,
fetch or build libraries. See `<MODULES>` in `wattle(7)`.

INFO FILE
=========

The ``info.edn`` file is known as the *info file*. It contains one map
describing the project. These are its keys:

- ``:name``:
  The project name. Application installation requires a name consisting of
  one directory name. An installed application name is unique across groups.

- ``:group``:
  The project group, by convention a reverse domain such as ``org.example``.
  Application installation requires this value. Like the application name,
  it cannot be empty, ``.`` or ``..``, or contain a path separator.

- ``:exes``:
  A vector of executable declarations. See `<EXECUTABLES>`.

- ``:libs``:
  A vector of library declarations. See `<LIBRARIES>`.

- ``:docs``:
  A vector of documentation declarations. See `<DOCUMENTATION>`.

- ``:vendored``:
  A vector describing sources copied into the project by
  ``wattle deps vendor``. See `wattle-dependencies(7)`.

Each key is optional. Commands that read artifact declarations require at
least one of ``:exes``, ``:libs`` or ``:docs``. An omitted artifact vector is
empty. Application installation requires ``:name`` and ``:group``.

Each artifact declaration is a map with a ``:type``. An executable or library
also has a ``:name`` consisting of letters, digits, ``_`` and ``-``. Names
are unique within each vector; an executable and a library may share a name.
A documentation declaration has no ``:name``.

A library or documentation declaration may have ``:for``, a vector of
executable names from the same project. It is part of each named executable.
Without ``:for``, it is not installed with an executable. A library cannot
name a web executable in ``:for``.

Paths in ``:files`` and the ``:root`` of a source library are relative to the
project and cannot contain ``..``. The old keys ``:artifacts`` and ``:man``
are rejected, as is ``:libs`` within an executable declaration.

EXECUTABLES
===========

Each declaration in ``:exes`` is a map containing ``:name``, ``:type`` and
``:entry`` keys. ``:entry`` is a path to the file that defines a function named
``main``. The executable types are:

- ``:native``:
  A standalone program built by ``wattle build exe``. The program includes the
  Wattle runtime, its compiled source and everything it imports. Native
  libraries whose ``:for`` names the executable are linked into it. Building
  requires the Zig toolchain.

- ``:source``:
  A program installed as Wattle source by ``wattle apps install``. It also
  requires ``:files``, a vector of the files and directories to install.
  ``wattle build`` does not build this type. Running the installed program
  requires Wattle. See `wattle-applications(7)`.

- ``:web``:
  A browser program built by ``wattle build web``. Building requires the Zig
  toolchain and produces a WebAssembly runtime, a program image and JavaScript.
  Web executables cannot have associated libraries and are not installed by
  ``wattle apps install``.

LIBRARIES
=========

Each entry of ``:libs`` has ``:name`` and ``:type``, and may have ``:for``.
A library provides modules for import. ``wattle build lib`` places libraries
on the project's module path, where a bare import uses the declared name.
The library types are:

- ``:source``:
  Wattle source. It requires ``:root``, one path to a file or directory in
  the project. A file is copied to ``.wattle/<name>.wattle``. The contents of
  a directory are copied to ``.wattle/<name>/``. A source library has no
  ``:files`` key.

- ``:native``:
  A native module. It requires ``:root``, the Zig root file. Building requires
  the Zig toolchain and produces a module in ``zig-out/lib``. A native library
  may also compile C into the module. ``:c-sources`` is a vector of paths to C
  files, ``:c-includes`` a vector of paths to include directories, and
  ``:c-flags`` a vector of flags for the C compiler, such as
  ``"-DHAVE_CONFIG_H"``. The paths are relative to the project. The same C
  files and flags are compiled into the shared library that ``wattle build
  lib`` produces and into each executable whose ``:for`` names the library.
  The Zig root declares the C functions it calls with ``extern fn``. A
  configuration header that depends on the target is a file in an include
  directory; nothing generates one.

- ``:remote``:
  A library from another project. It requires ``:url`` and ``:hash``, and may
  have ``:ref`` for a Git repository. The source project's info file must
  declare a library of the same name as ``:source`` or ``:native``. The
  library is placed in that form. See `wattle-dependencies(7)` for fetching
  and revision requirements.

For example, a source library with ``:name "util"`` and ``:root "lib/util"``
places `lib/util/init.wattle` at `.wattle/util/init.wattle`, imported as
``(import util)``. Its ``extra.wattle`` is imported as ``(import util/extra)``.
A file root is also imported by the declared name, regardless of its file
name in the project.

``wattle build exe`` will build the source and remote libraries that specify
the executable using ``:for``.

DOCUMENTATION
=============

Each entry of ``:docs`` has ``:type`` and ``:files``, a vector of paths, and
may have ``:for``. The documentation types are:

- ``:man``:
  Manual pages. Each file name ends in a period and a digit from 1 to 9. The
  number is its manual section. For example, ``man/hello.1`` is a man page that
  belongs in section 1.  Application installation copies the specified man
  pages into `share/man`.

- ``:web``:
  Web pages. ``wattle build web`` copies associated files beside the generated
  program. A web documentation declaration cannot name an installed executable
  in ``:for``.

EXAMPLES
========

A project with a native executable, a source library and a manual page:

````
{:name "greeter"
 :group "org.example"
 :exes [{:type :native
         :name "hello"
         :entry "main.wattle"}]
 :libs [{:type :source
         :name "util"
         :root "lib/util"
         :for ["hello"]}]
 :docs [{:type :man
         :files ["man/hello.1"]
         :for ["hello"]}]}
````

Build the executable and run the project's tests:

````
$ wattle build exe hello
$ wattle test
````

